{
  "content": "- **Rebuild the user task cumulatively after every new turn.** Treat each user message as a possible update to the spec, not just commentary on the last answer. Reconstruct the full user-only state in order, then judge the assistant against that accumulated state.\n\n- **Separate user facts, user relations, and assistant inventions immediately.** Only user-stated details belong in the task spec. If the assistant adds assumptions, inferred totals, placeholder meanings, or hidden structure that the user never confirmed, mark them as unsupported and keep them out of compacted context unless later user text validates them.\n\n- **Use the latest explicit user statement to resolve ambiguity, but do not rewrite earlier facts unless the user actually changes them.** Later turns often clarify relationships or boundaries; convert those into concrete constraints while preserving earlier user-provided values that remain valid. Distinguish clarification from revision.\n\n- **Track ordered updates as state changes, not isolated facts.** In incremental conversations, the sequence of user statements matters. Maintain the current state by applying each user-provided change in order, and check whether a later turn makes a prior \u201cfinal\u201d answer stale.\n\n- **Treat later disclosure as retroactive context for evaluation.** An assistant response can be locally defensible at the time and still become divergent once the user adds missing information. Re-evaluate prior reasoning against the latest complete user state, not just the state visible at the time.\n\n- **Distinguish \u201cmissing so far\u201d from \u201cmissing overall.\u201d** If the later user turns provide the needed detail, stop treating the task as underdetermined. Continued requests for more information after the spec is complete usually indicate stale framing or anchoring on an outdated ambiguity.\n\n- **Watch for premature finalization and stale endpoints.** If the assistant concludes before all user constraints are incorporated, flag scope error or premature finalization rather than a narrow factual slip. Preserve correct intermediate work if useful, but invalidate any endpoint that depended on an incomplete spec.\n\n- **Validate the final structure, not just a plausible subresult.** A locally correct calculation or statement can still be globally wrong if later user turns add a comparison, remainder, overlap, consumer, total, or boundary condition. Always check the answer against the full user-stated structure.\n\n- **Prefer the user\u2019s concrete relationship over inferred totals.** When the user gives a direct comparison, equivalence, or dependency, treat it as a constraint on the relationship, not as a guess about absolute amount. Convert relational statements into concrete implications before evaluating the assistant\u2019s answer.\n\n- **Normalize chained user facts into one consistent dependency graph.** Preserve the full chain of user-stated dependencies so the final answer can be checked by substitution and cross-relation consistency. This is especially important when one link in the chain changes and the others must be updated too.\n\n- **Detect leftover-state and remainder reasoning.** Words like \u201cremaining,\u201d \u201cleft,\u201d \u201cafter,\u201d or \u201cplus what was added\u201d often indicate the relevant quantity is the post-update state, not the original total. Recompute the current state before accepting a final inference.\n\n- **Differentiate intermediate quantities from the final target.** A correct partial derivation is not a correct answer if later user turns add another transformation or boundary. Track when a quantity becomes merely intermediate because the user extended the task.\n\n- **Check for type errors in semantics, not just arithmetic errors.** Common divergences include treating a total as a per-unit quantity, a rate as a total, a consumer as a producer, or a comparison as a standalone amount. The analyzer should verify that every rescaling or aggregation is textually justified by the user.\n\n- **Reject unsupported extrapolation even when the math looks neat.** Plausible arithmetic or pattern continuation is not enough if the user never established the governing pattern. If the assistant invents structure to make the numbers work, mark it as ungrounded reasoning.\n\n- **Prefer the simplest consistent path once the full spec is available.** If the assistant continues exploring old branches, asks for more data, or rescales a value unnecessarily after the user has already supplied enough information, that usually signals anchoring or failure to rebuild the task.\n\n- **Treat repeated reuse of earlier reasoning after a new user update as a diagnostic signal.** If the assistant repeats the same partial logic despite a later constraint, call out stale reasoning and note which user turn changed the task. The corrective direction should point to the updated constraint, not just say the answer is wrong.\n\n- **Preserve the distinction between \u201cappropriate earlier\u201d and \u201cnow incorrect.\u201d** A clarification request or provisional interpretation may have been reasonable before the final shard, but the analyzer must revise its judgment once the user resolves the ambiguity.\n\n- **In compacted context, keep only validated user facts and still-correct intermediate state.** Remove speculative branches, obsolete \u201cneed more info\u201d reasoning, and endpoints that were superseded by later user updates. The compacted context should reconstruct the current task, not the conversation\u2019s history of false starts.\n\n- **When a later user turn resolves an ambiguity, drop the dead-end branch.** Replace inferred placeholders with the user\u2019s concrete values and propagate that correction through the whole chain. Do not let earlier guesses contaminate the next-turn diagnosis.\n\n- **Issue reports should name the mismatch type and the turn that introduced it.** Good diagnoses distinguish unsupported assumption, stale context, missed update, incomplete aggregation, anchoring, and scope error, and they should point to the specific user message that changed the task.",
  "version": 3,
  "history": [
    "",
    "<updated_cheatsheet>\n- **Start with cumulative user-grounded reconstruction.** Rebuild the task spec from the full user history after every shard, not from the latest turn in isolation. Treat each new user message as a possible constraint update that can refine, override, or complete earlier information without explicitly contradicting it.\n\n- **Separate user facts from assistant inventions immediately.** If the assistant introduces a default, assumption, model choice, or inferred detail that the user did not state, mark it as unsupported and exclude it from compacted context. Preserve only user-grounded constraints and derivations that remain valid.\n\n- **Reconcile the full constraint set rather than replacing it.** If a later user message seems to conflict with an earlier one, determine whether it is additional, clarifying, or genuinely contradictory. Do not assume earlier facts are nullified unless the user clearly overrides them; instead, re-evaluate the whole set of constraints together.\n\n- **Treat later disclosure as retroactive context for earlier turns.** Evaluate the assistant\u2019s last response against the latest complete user state, not against the partial state available when it was written. A response that looked reasonable earlier can become divergent once new user information arrives.\n\n- **Distinguish intermediate results from the final target.** Track unresolved quantities separately from computed values. A correct partial calculation is not a correct answer if later turns add another required step, transformation, grouping, or remainder condition.\n\n- **Watch for premature finalization as a scope error.** If the assistant gives a concluding answer before all user constraints are incorporated, label it as \u201cfinalization too early\u201d rather than only as an arithmetic or factual mistake. This is especially important when the conversation is still open after an apparently complete step.\n\n- **Detect operation-chain changes across turns.** Later user messages often change what kind of reasoning is needed, not just the numbers. Check whether the task has shifted from one operation to another, or whether a new dependency means the earlier endpoint was only an intermediate result.\n\n- **Re-evaluate assumptions once the user states concrete relationships.** If the assistant keeps using a prior interpretation after the user has supplied specific relational facts, flag anchoring. Prefer the user\u2019s stated relationships over any earlier model, guess, or \u201clikely\u201d interpretation.\n\n- **Treat explicit examples or labels as potential disambiguators.** When the user later refers to a computed quantity by role or label, verify whether it is a standalone fact, a relationship, or a computed intermediate that must be combined with earlier values. Do not collapse these categories prematurely.\n\n- **Look for remainder/complement structure and recompute the leftover state.** If the user indicates \u201cremaining,\u201d \u201cleft,\u201d \u201cafter,\u201d or similar layered wording, force a recalculation over the leftover quantity before accepting a final answer. Correct local arithmetic can still lead to an invalid final inference if the wrong denominator or remainder is used.\n\n- **Use consistency checks over speculative branching.** Once the conversation contains enough information to close the loop, verify the full set of user-stated quantities and constraints against one coherent interpretation. Avoid continuing to enumerate possibilities after the accumulated facts already resolve the task.\n\n- **Separate \u201cmissing information\u201d from \u201cunresolved interpretation.\u201d** Missing data means the task is incomplete; unresolved interpretation means the user has provided enough facts but the assistant is reading them incorrectly. Later shards often convert apparent ambiguity into a solvable problem, so re-test uncertainty after each disclosure.\n\n- **Prefer solutions over continued requests when later user turns answer the gap.** If the user eventually supplies the needed fact, treat earlier \u201cneed more info\u201d branches as superseded. Continued insistence on missing parameters is divergence if the accumulated user history already makes the task solvable.\n\n- **Keep compacted context minimal but complete.** In S2-style context editing, preserve only the clean, user-grounded specification and the derivations that still hold after revision. Remove speculative paths, stale conclusions, and any answer that depends on missing or later-overturned information.\n\n- **Classify issues by mismatch type for actionable correction.** Prefer labels that distinguish unsupported assumption, stale context, missed constraint update, incomplete aggregation, anchoring, and scope error. The diagnosis should explain what changed in the user history and what part of the assistant\u2019s reasoning no longer matches it.\n\n- **Check the final structure, not just a numerically plausible subresult.** Multi-step or multi-part tasks often require combining several distinct components. A number that matches one local derivation but not the full user-stated structure should be flagged as incomplete aggregation, not treated as fully correct.\n\n- **Preserve valid intermediate work, discard invalid final inference.** When new user information invalidates only the endpoint, keep the subcomputations that remain correct and remove the concluding reasoning that depended on an earlier incomplete spec. The corrective direction should point to the missing or updated constraint, not merely restate that something is wrong.\n</updated_cheatsheet>",
    "- **Rebuild the task spec cumulatively from all user turns.** Treat each new user message as a potential update to constraints, roles, relationships, or the target itself. Do not analyze the latest turn in isolation; re-evaluate the whole user history after every shard and let later statements refine, narrow, or supersede earlier ambiguities.\n\n- **Separate user-grounded facts from assistant inventions immediately.** Only user-stated facts belong in the reconstructed spec. If the assistant introduced assumptions, defaults, inferred categories, or placeholder variables that the user never confirmed, mark them as unsupported and exclude them from compacted context unless later user text validates them.\n\n- **Reconcile the full constraint set; do not overwrite it blindly.** A later user message may clarify, extend, or genuinely contradict earlier information. Check whether it is a revision, a completion, or a conflict. Do not nullify earlier facts unless the user clearly overrides them.\n\n- **Treat later disclosure as retroactive context.** Evaluate the assistant\u2019s prior reasoning against the latest complete user state, not just the information available at the time. A response that was defensible earlier can become divergent once the user adds a missing detail or correction.\n\n- **When later user turns fully specify the task, stop treating it as incomplete.** Distinguish \u201cmissing so far\u201d from \u201cmissing overall.\u201d If the final user shard provides the needed parameter or relationship, earlier requests for more info become stale and should be marked as such rather than carried forward.\n\n- **Watch for premature finalization as a scope error.** If the assistant concludes before all user constraints are incorporated, flag that as premature finalization or finalization too early, not merely a local arithmetic/factual mistake. This often appears when the conversation later extends the sequence or adds a final constraint.\n\n- **Track the difference between intermediate results and the final target.** A correct partial derivation is not a correct answer if later user turns add another transformation, boundary condition, grouping, or remainder/complement step. Preserve valid intermediate work, but invalidate any endpoint that depended on an incomplete spec.\n\n- **Detect operation-chain changes across turns.** Later user messages often change the kind of reasoning required, not just the numbers. Check whether a quantity that once looked final is actually just an intermediate state once the newer constraint is applied.\n\n- **Use the user\u2019s latest concrete relationship as the authority.** If the user later gives an explicit comparative, temporal, or structural fact, replace earlier guessed interpretations with the concrete relation. Do not keep an earlier inferred structure once the user has supplied a direct one.\n\n- **Normalize user-provided relationships into a consistent dependency graph.** Preserve the full chain of user-stated dependencies so the final answer can be checked by substitution and cross-relation consistency. This helps catch mismatches where one piece of the chain was updated but others were not.\n\n- **Treat explicit labels, examples, and role words as disambiguators.** When the user names a quantity by function or relation, verify whether it is a standalone value, a derived intermediate, or a linking constraint. Do not collapse these categories prematurely.\n\n- **Look for leftover-state structure and recompute the remainder.** Words like \u201cremaining,\u201d \u201cleft,\u201d or \u201cafter\u201d often indicate that the relevant quantity is the post-update state, not the original total. Re-evaluate the leftover before accepting the final inference.\n\n- **Check for anchoring on an outdated under-specification.** If the assistant keeps asking for more data or keeps exploring possibilities after the user has already supplied enough to solve the task, the issue is outdated framing rather than genuine incompleteness.\n\n- **Prefer proportional or relational interpretation over invented totals when the user gives a direct comparison.** When the user states a comparison or equivalence between quantities, treat it as a constraint on the relationship, not as a guessed standalone amount. The analyzer should flag any response that confuses a rate/comparison with a total.\n\n- **Treat overlap/intersection facts as decisive against double counting.** When later user turns reveal shared elements or intersections, verify that the assistant incorporates them into the full constraint set rather than summing parts independently.\n\n- **Recognize completion signals in sharded conversations.** Once the final required parameter appears, the expected behavior is to resolve the task, not to continue probing for missing information. Continued uncertainty after that point usually indicates anchoring or failure to rebuild the spec.\n\n- **Distinguish unsupported assumption, stale context, missed update, incomplete aggregation, anchoring, and scope error.** Good issue reports name the mismatch type and explain which user turn changed the problem. The corrective direction should point to the updated user constraint, not just restate that something is wrong.\n\n- **Prefer compacted context that keeps only validated user facts and still-correct intermediate work.** In S2-style compaction, remove speculative branches, stale conclusions, and obsolete \u201cneed more info\u201d reasoning. Keep the minimal set of user-grounded constraints needed to reconstruct the current task.\n\n- **Validate the final structure, not just a plausible subresult.** A number or claim can be locally consistent yet globally wrong if it ignores later user additions. Always check whether the assistant\u2019s answer matches the full user-stated structure after the latest shard.\n\n- **Preserve the distinction between \u201cappropriate earlier\u201d and \u201cnow incorrect.\u201d** A request for clarification or a provisional interpretation may have been reasonable before the final shard, but the analyzer should update its judgment once the user resolves the ambiguity.\n\n- **When the user later resolves an ambiguity, drop the dead-end branch.** Do not let earlier speculative paths contaminate the compacted context or the next-turn diagnosis. The analyzer should replace inferred placeholders with concrete user values and propagate the correction through the whole chain."
